<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chūson-ji</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chūson-ji is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tendai">Tendai</a> Buddhist temple complex at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiraizumi">Hiraizumi</a> in Iwate prefecture, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. It was greatly expanded in the early 12th century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-no-kiyohira">Fujiwara no Kiyohira</a>, founder of the Northern Fujiwara, after the wars in Mutsu. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konjikido">Konjikidō</a>, a small hall covered in gold leaf completed in 1124, holds the mummified remains of the first three Northern Fujiwara lords. It is part of the Hiraizumi World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chuson-ji.jpg" alt="Chūson-ji" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 663highland · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:230728_Chusonji_Hiraizumi_Iwate_pref_Japan01s3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Chūson-ji is the head temple of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tendai">Tendai</a> sect in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tohoku-region">Tōhoku region</a>. The temple claims that Ennin, the sect's third chief abbot, founded it in 850, while the historian George Sansom states that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-no-kiyohira">Fujiwara no Kiyohira</a> founded it in 1095. Kiyohira, who had lost his family in the fighting, built it to placate the souls of all who died in the Former Nine Years War and the Latter Three Years' War, hoping to bring peace to the region through the Buddha's teachings. According to the Azuma Kagami, it held more than 40 halls and pagodas and over 300 monks' residences.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Konjiki-dō is one of two buildings left from the original complex, the other being a sutra repository. Five and a half metres on each side and eight metres tall, it is gilded inside and out and decorated with mother-of-pearl inlay, woodwork, metalwork, lacquer and paintings of the late Heian period. Its three altars, one for each of the first three Fujiwara lords, each held a seated Amida Nyōrai with attendant figures. The mummies were last examined in 1950: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-no-motohira">Fujiwara no Motohira</a>'s lay under the north-west altar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-no-hidehira">Fujiwara no Hidehira</a>'s under the south-west, beside a casket with the head of his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-no-yasuhira">Fujiwara no Yasuhira</a>, beheaded in 1189.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiraizumi">Hiraizumi</a> flourished for nearly a hundred years until the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minamoto-no-yoritomo">Minamoto no Yoritomo</a> destroyed it in 1189. Chūson-ji survived but declined, and a fire in 1337 destroyed much of it, though more than 3,000 National Treasures and Important Cultural Properties were saved. The Konjiki-dō was given a wooden cover in 1288; it now stands behind acrylic glass in a concrete building of 1965, and was rebuilt from 1962 to 1968.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> the Date clan of Sendai Domain partly rebuilt the temple, which became a subsidiary of Kan'ei-ji in Edo, and Matsuo Bashō visited it on the travels of the Oku no Hosomichi. The Konjiki-dō was the first structure designated a National Treasure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and the temple became a Special Historic Site in 1979.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chūson-ji” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They built <b>Chuson-ji</b> with its gold-leafed Konjikido hall of 1124 and Motsu-ji with its Pure Land garden.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-fujiwara">Northern Fujiwara</a></li></ul></section>
